```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
The fault is a missing BGP configuration on router2 — not a problem with router1,
which is fully healthy.

Affected component: The BGP daemon (bgpd) on router2 and the entire router bgp
configuration stanza that should define its role in AS 2.

Mechanism: router1 is correctly configured in AS 1 with `neighbor 193.10.11.2
remote-as 2` and advertises 195.11.14.0/24. However, router2's running
configuration contains nothing beyond the bare FRR boilerplate — there is no
`router bgp` block, no neighbor statement, and no network advertisement.
Consequently, FRR never started bgpd on router2 (`bgpd is not running`), so
router1's repeated BGP OPEN attempts are never answered and its session stays
permanently in the `Active` state. With no BGP session established, neither
router learns the remote prefix: router1 has no route to 200.1.1.0/24 and
router2 has no route to 195.11.14.0/24. All packets from pc1 (195.11.14.2) to
pc2 (200.1.1.2) are black-holed at router1.

Localization: router2, global BGP process — the entire `router bgp 2` stanza is
absent from the running configuration.

Root cause: router2's BGP configuration was never applied (or was wiped),
leaving bgpd unconfigured and unstarted.

Corrective fix: On router2, enter vtysh and configure:
  `router bgp 2`, `neighbor 193.10.11.1 remote-as 1`, `no bgp
  ebgp-requires-policy`, and `network 200.1.1.0/24` under `address-family ipv4
  unicast`. Once bgpd starts and the eBGP session with router1 reaches
  Established, both prefixes will be exchanged and pc1↔pc2 connectivity will be
  restored. Restarting router1 would have no effect.
=== END ===
```